    "body": "Held by maintainer decision (2026-11-25). This issue waits for the load control step of the new path kernel issue. The held work is on branch `issue/3933-pocket-offset-rings-spiral` at `458c6ee82c1dcb4fc13adaef9007510a1fb684bd`. There is no PR.\n\n## What I learned\n\n- The screenshot case (round pocket, Pass starts at Inside, no hole) goes through `plan_passes` -> `waterline_passes`. It cuts closed rings at `optimal_load x D`. A straight chord joins each ring to the next ring. This chord is a radial step into the material at up to full width.\n- The step is read one time for each `generate` (`tiling_step()`). It is not read again for each level or ring.\n- On a round pocket, rings at a constant step do not give a constant load. Near a small core the load is much higher (Stori & Wright 2000, circular path law). Read on the HSM planner's stock: 49.6 % of D for a 25 % load at uniform spacing.\n- Sharp ring corners load the tool past the limit in any offset spiral (for example 94 % of D on a rectangle). Only a plan on the stock (Adaptive) holds the load there.\n\n## Design on the branch\n\n- New `machining2d/offset_law.rs`: ring depths from the engagement law. The step holds the load's contact angle on the tightest round stretch of each ring. The core ring stands a law radius in from the deepest point.\n- New `machining2d/offset_spiral.rs`: each ring morphs into the next ring out (`adaptive::spiral::revolution`, Held & Spielberger 2009). The wall ring is cut last as one full lap. A morph is used only if it stays in the band between two adjacent rings (no crossing). Else the proved chord stays.\n- Guards: a completeness check (more than 0.1 mm2 unswept gives the old rings, with a note). A load read on the planner's stock (`adaptive/reading.rs`) names the worst load and a long core slot.\n- Scope: 2D Pocket and the 2D Adaptive ring fallback only. The 3-D ladder and the Inside/Outside level pieces do not change. The decision is in `docs/decisions/3933-pocket-offset-rings-spiral.md`.\n\n## Readings (motion unit fixtures, tool r = 2 mm, 25 % load)\n\n| Fixture | Plan | Passes | Cut length | Worst load after the core |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Disc, radius 10 | old rings | 1 | 354.5 mm | 100 % (ring step) |\n| Disc, radius 10 | spiral | 1 | 510.9 mm | 25.2 % |\n| Rectangle 30 x 20 | old rings | 1 | 670.3 mm | 100 % |\n| Rectangle 30 x 20 | spiral | 1 | 692.8 mm | 93.9 % (corners, named) |\n\n## Test state on the branch\n\n- Green: `offset_spiral` and `offset_law` unit tests, `corner_arc_radius`, `machining2d::offset_rings_are_the_engagement_law_on_the_plane`, `xy_arc_entry`. Pins changed: the last three (reasons are in the test comments).\n- Not run after the last change: `pick_agnostic` (was red at a 16.0 % read for a 14.6 % load before the core change), the full quick gate.\n- Red: `example_catalog` `catalog_multiaxis_indexed` (it uses `offset_rings`). Its `.nc` and `.camproj` must be rebuilt with `cargo xtask catalog --case multiaxis/indexed`.\n- Known loss: with an XY arc entry on a round pocket, the arc does not fit beside the core start (less room than one sixteenth radius before the first revolution). The entry descends without the arc, and a note says so.\n\nNo pass that can gouge the part or a fixture was found on main.\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
